# {{name}}

Automated acceptance tests for a Windows desktop app. Scenarios are written in plain English
(Gherkin) in `features/`; the code behind each step lives in one shared library in `steps/`.
They run with [pytest-bdd](https://pytest-bdd.readthedocs.io) and drive the app through
Windows UI Automation, the accessibility interface screen readers use (`uia.py`, with the
`comtypes` package).

Run them on a test VM, not on a machine with real work open: UI Automation can press buttons
in any window the logged-in user can see.

## Set up (once, on Windows)

You need Python 3.11 or newer.

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock -r requirements-desktop.lock
```

`--require-hashes` installs exactly the versions in the lock files and refuses any file that
doesn't match its recorded hash.

## Check your app can be tested this way

Open the app, start Narrator (Win + Ctrl + Enter) and Tab through it. If Narrator reads out
the names of its buttons and fields, the steps can use those names. Press Win + Ctrl + Enter
again to stop Narrator.

## Run the tests

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\python -m pytest                # every scenario
.venv\Scripts\python -m pytest -k checkbox    # only scenarios whose name matches
```

The example in `features/character_map.feature` tests Character Map, which every Windows has,
so it proves the setup works on this machine. Delete it once your first real scenario passes.
On macOS or Linux the scenarios are skipped: they need Windows.

## Point it at your app

Set `desktop_app` in `pytest.ini` to the app's `.exe`. If the app starts through a launcher or
a splash screen, also set `desktop_window` to part of its main window's title. For one run,
the `DESKTOP_APP` environment variable overrides `desktop_app`.

Each scenario starts the app and closes it afterwards, even if the scenario fails. If the app
is already open, the scenario stops rather than take over (and later close) that window. Apps
that need administrator rights need the tests started as administrator.

## Steps you can use

| Step | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Given the app is open` | Starts the app and waits for its window |
| `When I click "Save"` | Presses a button (also checkboxes, options, tabs, list and menu items) |
| `When I tick "Remember me"` | Ticks a checkbox, if it isn't ticked already |
| `When I type "ACME Ltd" into "Customer name"` | Fills in a field |
| `Then "Customer name" shows "ACME Ltd"` | Checks a field's text |
| `Then "Customer name" is not empty` | Checks a field has something in it |
| `Then "Remember me" is ticked` | Checks a checkbox |
| `Then I see "Customer saved"` | Checks some text appears anywhere in the app, message boxes included |

Controls are found by the name Narrator reads out (a trailing colon doesn't matter), or by the
developer's AutomationId when a name is missing or used twice. When a control can't be found,
the error lists every control the app shows.

## Add a scenario

1. Write it in a `.feature` file in `features/`. Every scenario there runs automatically.
2. Run `gherkin-copilot check features --stubs`. It lists every line that has no step yet and
   drafts the code for it.
3. Add new steps to a file in `steps/`, grouped by area (`login_steps.py`,
   `invoice_steps.py`...). Every file in `steps/` is loaded automatically.

Or use the app: run `gherkin-copilot ui` and add this folder as a project (steps folder
`steps`, features folder `features`). A line with no step gets **Build its code with action
blocks**: click, tick, type into, check what a field shows, check text appears. No Python
needed.

## Conventions

- **One shared step library.** Before writing a step, search for one that already exists:
  `gherkin-copilot find "When I click"`. Never copy a step into a new file for one scenario.
- **Say what, not how.** `Given I am logged in as a clerk`, not five typing steps. The step's
  code knows the details, so a changed login window means one fix.
- **`Given` sets the scene, `When` acts, `Then` only checks.**
- **Keep the library tidy.** `gherkin-copilot dupes` finds steps that say the same thing in
  different words, and `gherkin-copilot merge` combines them.

## In CI

Desktop tests need a Windows machine with a desktop session, such as a Windows runner; a
Linux Docker runner can't run them. On a Windows runner:

```powershell
python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock -r requirements-desktop.lock
gherkin-copilot check features          # fails if a line has no step, or matches two
python -m pytest --cucumberjson=reports/cucumber.json
```
